Consider the low-energy interaction of an antikaon ($K^-$) with nucleons. Define the complex $K^-p$ scattering length $a_{K^-p}=\Re a_{K^-p}+i\,\Im a_{K^-p}$ (in fm), the corresponding $K^-n$ scattering length $a_{K^-n}$, and the isospin-decomposed antikaon–nucleon scattering lengths $a_0$ and $a_1$ (in the $I=0$ and $I=1$ channels), related by $a_{K^-p}=\tfrac12(a_0+a_1)$ and $a_{K^-n}=a_1$. Let $f_{\bar K N}(\sqrt{s})$ denote the coupled-channel $\bar K N\to\bar K N$ scattering amplitudes (with channels $\bar K N$, $\pi\Sigma$, $\pi\Lambda$, $\eta\Lambda$, $\eta\Sigma$, $K\Xi$) for center-of-mass energies $\sqrt{s}$ in a subthreshold region (down to roughly $35\,$MeV below the $\bar K N$ threshold), and let the energy shift $\Delta E_{1s}$ and width $\Gamma_{1s}$ of the $1s$ state of a kaonic atom be related to the corresponding kaonic-atom scattering length through a Deser-type formula including isospin-breaking and recoil corrections.

Determine values of $\Re a_{K^-p}$, $\Im a_{K^-p}$, $a_{K^-n}$, and $a_0,a_1$, together with the subthreshold $\bar K N$ amplitudes $f_{\bar K N}(\sqrt{s})$, that are simultaneously consistent with:
(i) the measured $1s$ energy shift and width of kaonic hydrogen;
(ii) the measured $1s$ shift and width of kaonic deuterium (after solving the $K^-pn$ three-body problem with full coupled-channel dynamics);
(iii) low-energy $K^-p\to K^-p,\bar K^0 n,\pi^\pm\Sigma^\mp,\pi^0\Sigma^0,\pi^0\Lambda$ cross-section data;
(iv) the measured $\pi\Sigma$ invariant-mass spectra in the $\Lambda(1405)$ region.

Specify how the above empirical inputs constrain the analytic continuation of $f_{\bar K N}(\sqrt{s})$ to the subthreshold region used as input in calculations of kaonic nuclei (e.g.\ kaonic $^3$He, $^4$He, and putative deeply-bound $\bar K$-nuclear states).

